# `can`, `trusted`, `defer` and `parallel` are CONTEXTUAL keywords — ordinary identifiers
# everywhere except at the head of a block — and stage1 rejected valid programs that used them
# as variable names. stage0 accepts every line below.
#
# The statement dispatcher had the right guard and applied it to ONE name: "a DOT or an
# assignment arrow after the name is a use of the value, never a block" was computed for
# `scope` only, while the `if` beneath it claimed the statement for seven names. `can` died
# with a parse error on the rebind; `trusted`, `defer` and `parallel` were worse — the parse
# "succeeded" with the declaration silently eaten, which is the same failure mode that
# produced 319 false diagnostics when `scope` was unguarded.
#
# The DECLARATION line is what actually breaks first, and it needs its own test: `NAME: T = v`
# has a colon where a block header also has one. A block header's colon ENDS the line, so the
# token after it separates them.
#
# Two boundaries the fix must not cross, both measured:
#   * `trusted [Unsafe.PointerCast, …]:` is a real block form whose grant list is BRACKETED.
#     Treating a bracket as a use across the board turned every one of those into a parse
#     error and took 12 of the 574 stage0 semantic-acceptance cases with it. A bracket means
#     "use" for `scope` alone, where `scope[i]` is an index.
#   * stage0 RESERVES `scope` and `nursery` in statement position — it rejects
#     `scope: mutable i64 = 1` — so those two must keep claiming the statement.
#
# stage0 answers 64.
def sink(v: i64) -> i64:
    return v


def main() -> i64:
    can: mutable i64 = 1
    can <- can + 1

    trusted: mutable i64 = 2
    trusted <- trusted + 1

    defer: mutable i64 = 3
    defer <- defer + 1

    parallel: mutable i64 = 4
    parallel <- parallel + 1

    return sink(can) + sink(trusted) * 2 + sink(defer) * 4 + sink(parallel) * 8
